[0.000, "o", "                                               \r\n \u001b[1;38;2;127;147;166mcommandlineuser.com\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                   \r\n                                                                                                                                                       \r\n   \u001b[38;2;233;239;243mCommand Line User is a catalogue of command-line tools for developers — and anyone else who spends time in a terminal. It ingests from multiple\u001b[0m     \r\n \u001b[38;2;233;239;243msources nightly, scores each item on utility, maturity, maintenance, and confidence, and presents the results without hype.\u001b[0m                           \r\n                                                                                                                                                       \r\n \u001b[1;38;2;127;147;166mAbout\u001b[0m                                                                                                          "]
[0.000, "o", "                                       \r\n                                                                                                                                                       \r\n   \u001b[38;2;233;239;243mI'm Alister. I've been a Unix and Linux user most of my working life, and a terminal habitué for longer than I care to work out. I use bash, and\u001b[0m    \r\n \u001b[38;2;233;239;243mhave come to depend on CLI utilities I've accumulated across two decades of keeping dotfiles.\u001b[0m                                                         \r\n   \u001b[38;2;233;239;243mProfessionally I've worked across development, operations, design, and product, but always leaned toward the command line to automate what I was\u001b[0m    \r\n \u001b[38;2;233;239;243mdoing. As a product designer I was fascinated by developer experience and how CLI and TUI tools could enhance it.\u001b[0m                                     \r\n   \u001b[38;2;233;239;243mInstead of trawling GitHub trends, stale Awesome lists, and Reddit threads, I built this to surface and"]
[0.000, "o", " score tools I'd actually want to use.\u001b[0m       \r\n   \u001b[38;2;233;239;243mThe feature articles on this site are written to reflect my own experience using certain CLI tools. Not tutorials. Just what I noticed.\u001b[0m             \r\n                                                                                                                                                       \r\n \u001b[1;38;2;127;147;166mHow the catalogue works\u001b[0m                                                                                                                               \r\n                                                                                                                                                       \r\n   \u001b[38;2;233;239;243mScoring is automated and transparent. Every catalogue item carries a score, a confidence value, and an explanation of what signals drove both.\u001b[0m      \r\n                                                                                                                                         "]
[0.000, "o", "              \r\n   \u001b[38;2;168;182;192mItems that fall short — abandoned projects, out-of-scope packages, things with unclear provenance — are excluded via a maintained blocklist and a\u001b[0m   \r\n \u001b[38;2;168;182;192mreview queue checked before each site publish.\u001b[0m                                                                                                        \r\n                                                                                                                                                       \r\n \u001b[1;38;2;127;147;166mLinks\u001b[0m                                                                                                                                                 \r\n                                                                                                                                                       \r\n   \u001b[1;38;2;127;147;166mAbout:\u001b[0m          \u001b[38;2;168;182;192mhttps://commandlineuser.com/about/\u001b[0m                                                                   "]
